  {/* ──────────────── 8 · METHODOLOGY & AMENDMENTS ──────────────── */}
  <section class="bb-section bb-section--mercury" id="methodology" aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="container container--wide">
      <header class="bb-sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--mercury">Reproducibility</span>
        <h2 id="method-h" class="bb-sec-h">Methodology &amp; amendments.</h2>
        <p class="bb-sec-lead">
          Pre-registered before the v2 matrix ran; the plan's SHA-256 is embedded in every report
          so post-hoc deviation is detectable. Eleven logged amendments track every mid-flight
          change.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="bb-methgrid">
        <div class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>The pre-registered plan.</h3>
          <ul class="bb-facts">
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Unit of inference</span>
              <span>
                the <strong>task cluster</strong> — trials within a task are averaged, never
                treated as independent
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Primary test</span>
              <span>
                two-sided <strong>sign-flip permutation</strong> on paired task-cluster means;
                exact enumeration ≤20 clusters, Monte Carlo <strong>200,000</strong> reps
                otherwise (seed 20260806)
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Multiplicity</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Holm step-down</strong> within pre-registered families: pooled harness,
                coding subgroup, model pairs
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Confidence</span>
              <span>
                <strong>{params.ci === "wilson-95" ? "Wilson 95%" : (params.ci ?? "Wilson 95")}</strong>
                intervals on every published rate; n &lt; {params.medium_n ?? 10} ⇒ low
                confidence, ineligible for top rank
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Shrinkage</span>
              <span>
                slices with n &lt; {params.high_n ?? 40} pool toward the stack overall, then the
                global rate (prior strength {params.prior_strength ?? 10}) — labeled
                <strong> inferred</strong>, never presented as measured
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Isolation</span>
              <span>
                <strong>split-phase-v1</strong>: agent and verifier run in separate containers;
                the oracle bundle mounts read-only only in the verify phase
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Wall-clock caps</span>
              <span>
                1,500s hard batch · 1,800s standard; strict rule <code>timed_out ⇒ fail</code>
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="bb-facts__k">Provenance</span>
              <span>
                per-cell image digests in meta.json; runs <code>{shaShort}…</code>;
                {" "}{stats.excluded} cells excluded by policy, {fmtInt(stats.trials)} measured
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Amendment log.</h3>
          <ol class="bb-amend">
            {
              AMENDMENTS.map(([title, date, body], i) => (
                <li class="bb-amend__item">
                  <h4>
                    Amendment {i + 1} — {title}
                    <span class="mono">{date}</span>
                  </h4>
                  <p>{body}</p>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bb-method bb-method--spaced">
        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>The suite.</h3>
          <p>
            Tasks follow the <code>NN_name</code> convention and are tagged by family, language,
            framework, platform, and difficulty. Thirteen families span the work agents actually
            get asked to do: <code>refactor</code>, <code>bugfix</code>, <code>feature</code>,
            <code>performance</code>, <code>systems</code>, <code>parser</code>,
            <code>agentic-ops</code>, and the experiential families — <code>visual-svg</code>,
            <code>visual-canvas</code>, <code>visual-3d</code>, <code>game</code>,
            <code>sim-game</code>, <code>ui-build</code>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Two validators.</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Script-validated</strong> tasks run a <code>verify.sh</code> pass/fail gate.
            <strong>Human-validated</strong> tasks (every experiential family) run mechanical
            packaging checks, then go to the <a href="/bench/arena">Arena</a> for blind pairwise
            judgment — a script can't grade whether a game feels finished.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Strict rate vs solution rate.</h3>
          <p>
            The <strong>solution rate</strong> credits a cell when the deliverable solves the
            task under the suite's solution checks. The <strong>strict rate</strong> is the
            harsher gate: every mechanical check green, no partial credit, no packaging
            exceptions. The gap between them is visible on every leaderboard bar — a wide gap
            means a stack ships almost-right work.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Statistics you can audit.</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Every rate carries a <strong>Wilson 95% confidence interval</strong> at the cell's n.</li>
            <li>
              <code>n &lt; 10</code> ⇒ <strong>low confidence</strong>, and the stack may not be
              ranked first.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Inferred</strong> cells are shrinkage-pooled estimates — shown dashed,
              never presented as measured.
            </li>
            <li>
              Arena ratings are <strong>Bradley-Terry</strong> with bootstrap CIs, published only
              at 30+ votes per stack.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Provenance &amp; freshness.</h3>
          <p>
            Each export pins <code>generated_at_utc</code> and a <code>runs_sha256</code> over the
            underlying run set, so consumers can reject stale files. This build:
            <strong>{generatedAt}</strong>, runs <code>{shaShort}…</code>,
            {fmtInt(BENCH.source.cells_measured)} cells measured,
            {fmtInt(BENCH.source.cells_excluded)} excluded (superseded or invalid), trees
            <code>{BENCH.source.trees.map((t) => t.split("/").pop()).join(", ")}</code>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>How medians are computed.</h3>
          <p>
            <code>wall_seconds_median</code>, <code>cost_usd_median</code>, and
            <code>tokens_median</code> are
            <strong>n-weighted medians of per-task medians</strong> (<code>params.scope_medians</code>):
            each task contributes its own median, weighted by its trial count, so the mix of
            fast and slow tasks doesn't drag the number. A pooled run-level median will differ —
            pi × GLM 5.2 shows 43.9s task-weighted against 52.7s pooled.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Cost &amp; speed.</h3>
          <p>
            <code>cost_usd_median</code> is the median per-task dollar cost at configured list
            prices; <code>wall_seconds_median</code> and <code>tokens_median</code> are medians
            over the same cells. Every row declares <code>cost_source</code>:
            <strong> measured</strong> when the harness reported a monetary figure (or the model
            is configured free), <strong> derived</strong> when cost was computed from token
            counts × per-million list prices — cached input tokens billed at the full input rate,
            a disclosed upper bound for cache-heavy stacks — and <strong> none</strong> when no
            cost evidence exists. Derived costs are estimates, never receipts. Subsidized models
            additionally carry <code>cost_standard_usd_median</code>: the muse-spark contributor
            tier is free under a data-sharing agreement, so every contributor cell is repriced at
            the standard non-contributor rates (<code>meta/muse-spark-1.2</code>, $1.25/M input,
            $4.25/M output) and shown alongside the $0.00 — the subsidy is disclosed, never
            presented as the model's price. The
            <a href="/bench#pareto">Pareto view</a> plots cost against quality so a cheap stack
            that almost ships is priced honestly against an expensive one that does.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Run integrity.</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Timeouts.</strong> Every task runs against a per-task wall cap; timed-out
              runs count as failures and stay in the wall medians — a median is robust to them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>No-op runs.</strong> A run whose final diff touches only harness
              scaffolding — zero source edits — is a no-op, counted as a failure and flagged
              wherever its cell surfaces.
              {noop && noop.runs > 0 && (
                <>
                  {" "}
                  {fmtInt(noop.runs)} {noop.runs === 1 ? "run" : "runs"} across {noop.cells}
                  {noop.cells === 1 ? "cell" : "cells"} ended with no source edits — counted as
                  failures, flagged for transparency.
                </>
              )}
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Token accounting.</strong> 2026-08-08: fixed an opencode step-usage parser
              that could undercount output tokens; two historical runs were repaired from event
              logs. Published token medians use <code>task_total_tokens</code> and were
              unaffected.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="bb-methcard">
          <h3>Reasoning effort parity.</h3>
          <p>
            Every run requested reasoning effort <code>high</code>. Delivery differs by harness:
            claude, pi, omp, and prime-agent carry an explicit flag; the others reach it through
            config or alias; opencode had no explicit delivery path in this run set and is
            flagged for rerun. Treat cross-harness effort as a known limitation of this export.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      {
        /* Suite coverage + excluded tasks — render only once the export ships
           its coverage block; older exports hide the whole block cleanly. */
        coverage && (
          <div class="bb-cov">
            <article class="bb-methcard">
              <h3>Suite coverage.</h3>
              <p>
                Each model's measured task set, from the export's <code>coverage</code> block.
                {coverage.commonCount > 0 &&
                  `${coverage.commonCount} tasks are common to all ${coverage.models.length} models — the like-for-like comparison scope.`}
                Harness gaps list tasks the model ran on other harnesses but not that one.
              </p>
              <div class="bb-cov__wrap">
                <table class="bb-data bb-cov__table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">model</th>
                      <th scope="col">tasks</th>
                      <th scope="col">families</th>
                      <th scope="col">harness gaps</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {coverage.models.map((m) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td class={`d-model m--${mcls(m.id)}`}>{modelDisplay(m.id)}</td>
                        <td>{m.tasks}</td>
                        <td class="d-sub bb-cov__fams">{m.families.join(", ")}</td>
                        <td class="d-sub bb-cov__gaps">
                          {m.gaps.length === 0
                            ? "none"
                            : m.gaps
                                .map(
                                  (g) =>
                                    `${harnessDisplay(g.harness)} missing ${g.missing.join(", ")}`
                                )
                                .join(" · ")}
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              {coverage.minTasks !== coverage.maxTasks && (
                <p class="bb-note bb-cov__note">
                  <strong>Cross-model overall rates pool different suites.</strong>
                  {`${coverage.maxModels.map(modelDisplay).join(" and ")} ran ${coverage.maxTasks} tasks${coverage.extraFamilies.length > 0 ? ` (families incl. ${coverage.extraFamilies.join("/")})` : ""}; ${coverage.minModels.map(modelDisplay).join(" and ")} ran the ${coverage.minTasks}-task systems suite. Use the`}
                  <strong>Systems family</strong>
                  {`scope — common to all ${coverage.models.length} models — for apples-to-apples model comparison.`}
                </p>
              )}
            </article>

            {coverage.excluded.length > 0 && (
              <article class="bb-methcard">
                <h3>Excluded tasks.</h3>
                <div class="bb-cov__wrap">
                  <table class="bb-data bb-cov__table bb-cov__table--excl">
                    <thead>
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="col">task</th>
                        <th scope="col">reason</th>
                        <th scope="col">disposition</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      {coverage.excluded.map((x) => (
                        <tr>
                          <td>{x.task}</td>
                          <td class="d-sub bb-cov__reason">{x.reason}</td>
                          <td class="d-sub">excluded from rates, retained in coverage</td>
                        </tr>
                      ))}
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </article>
            )}
          </div>
        )
      }

      <p class="bb-note bb-note--spaced">
        <strong>The pipeline's self-contained report.</strong> The BurnBench pipeline also emits a
        single-file interactive HTML report from the same evidence store. This page is that
        report's native port — same export, every number recomputed at build time, rendered inside
        burnbar.ai's hash-locked CSP with zero inline runtime. The raw artifact stays in the
        pipeline repo for offline reading; the JSON projections behind this page are served at
        <a href="/data/bench-report.json"><code>/data/bench-report.json</code></a> and
        <a href="/data/bench-cells.json"><code>/data/bench-cells.json</code></a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

